Housing instability
Social & Environmental
Frequent forced moves, Unsafe living conditions, SDOH screening indicating precarious housing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-018
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of pediatric asthma directly linked to highly unsafe physical living conditions.
Location: Temporary motel accommodation
Present at Visit: Patient (child), mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a child with acute, worsening shortness of breath and continuous coughing in a mold-infested temporary motel room. Urgent assessment was required to reverse acute bronchospasm and prevent respiratory failure prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He can't stop coughing and his chest is sucking in; this room is full of black mold."
History of Present Illness: The mother reports a 24-hour history of progressively worsening wheeze, tachypnea, and continuous nocturnal coughing in her 8-year-old son. Key symptoms stem from severe housing instability, characterized by frequent forced moves and evictions. They currently reside in overcrowded, unsafe physical living conditions (a substandard motel). The child is experiencing a severe exacerbation of asthma due directly to visible mold and poor ventilation. The mother reports worsening of the child's chronic disease due to a lack of stable medication storage and disrupted continuous primary care. Both patient and mother exhibit elevated physiological stress markers.
Functional Status: Child is acutely distressed and limited by dyspnea
Social/Safety Concerns: Toxic environmental exposure, precarious housing status, high risk of recurrent acute hospital admissions

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 135 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 32 | SpO2: 91% | Wt: 28 Kg
General: Child is sitting upright, visibly distressed, utilizing accessory muscles (intercostal retractions), unable to speak in full sentences.
Physical Exam: Respiratory auscultation reveals widespread, tight expiratory wheeze and a markedly prolonged expiratory phase with decreased air entry at the bases. Cardiovascular exam shows sinus tachycardia. Diagnostic tetrad features are present: frequent forced moves, unsafe living conditions, and SDOH indicators of precarious housing.
Environmental Observations: Strong, musty odor in the room, visible black mold on the bathroom ceiling and window frames, damp carpet.
Medication Reconciliation: Preventer inhaler (fluticasone) empty for two weeks due to lost prescription during the last move.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Housing instability (Acute severe pediatric asthma exacerbation) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Viral Bronchiolitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Age of the child (8 years old) and the classic presentation of acute bronchospasm rather than a primary upper respiratory tract infection progressing to lower tract crackles.
2. Foreign Body Aspiration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of bilateral, diffuse wheeze rather than unilateral decreased breath sounds, and a clear history of worsening symptoms linked to mold exposure.
3. Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of fever, productive purulent cough, and focal consolidation on auscultation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The pediatric patient presented with an acute, severe asthma exacerbation, evidenced by significant hypoxemia (SpO2 91%), pronounced tachypnea, and accessory muscle use, directly precipitated by severe environmental mold in precarious housing. Delaying medical intervention until standard hours would have posed a life-threatening risk of respiratory exhaustion and arrest. The objective signs of respiratory compromise completely justify the urgent after-hours intervention to immediately administer high-dose bronchodilators and systemic corticosteroids to reverse the airway obstruction.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate administration of nebulized salbutamol and ipratropium bromide. Administration of oral prednisolone (stat dose). Provision of a spacer and new salbutamol inhaler from clinician supplies.
Positive Behavioral Change: By using the preventer inhaler every single morning and night, even when breathing feels fine, you build a protective shield in his lungs that stops the mold from triggering these dangerous asthma attacks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to a housing support worker/medical-legal partnership to mandate emergency relocation from the toxic environment.
DME/Safety (Optional): None
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 24 hours to assess respiratory status and review the asthma action plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the child's breathing does not improve within 20 minutes of the nebulizer, if he becomes lethargic, or if you see the skin sucking in deeply at his neck or ribs again.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute asthma attack will improve with the steroids and relievers, but will inevitably recur if the child remains in the mold-infested environment.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if his lips turn blue, if he is too breathless to talk, or if the reliever puffer stops working.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The mold in this room has severely inflamed your son's airways. The nebulizer opens them up quickly, and the steroid liquid reduces the swelling over the next few days.
Actionable Steps: Administer the steroid medication exactly as prescribed for the next 3 days. Keep the bathroom door closed and the window open if possible. Contact the emergency housing number provided first thing in the morning.

